🔹 Phase 1: SRE Bootcamp Training

During the initial phase of my internship, I participated in an intensive SRE (Site Reliability Engineering) Bootcamp, where I gained hands-on experience with multiple DevOps tools and technologies.

Key Tools & Technologies:

Programming & APIs: Python, REST API, Postman

Containerization: Docker, Docker Compose

Orchestration: Kubernetes, Helm Charts, Multinode Cluster Setup

Version Control & CI/CD: Git, GitHub, GitHub Actions, Tekton, FluxCD

Secrets & Storage: Vault, External Secrets Store

Database: PostgreSQL

Infrastructure: On-premises cluster setup, Air-gapped environment

This bootcamp focused on real-world DevOps practices — from containerization and orchestration to automation, CI/CD, and observability.
..................................................................................................................................................
🔹 Phase 2: Real-Time Project — Microservices Deployment in Air-Gapped Environment

After completing the bootcamp, I was assigned a real-time project for the client Global Logic.
The goal was to deploy microservices on an on-premises, air-gapped Kubernetes environment — meaning the environment had no internet access.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔸 Task 1: Kubernetes Setup for Databases

My first task was to automate the deployment of PostgreSQL and Kafka on the Kubernetes cluster using Bitnami Helm charts.
I wrote a script to handle the entire deployment process, ensuring that it could run efficiently in an air-gapped setup.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔸 Task 2: CI/CD Architecture Setup

Initially, our team planned to use Tekton for both Continuous Integration (CI) and Continuous Deployment (CD).
Later, based on client requirements, we finalized:

Tekton for CI

FluxCD for CD

This allowed better separation of the build and deployment processes.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔸 Task 3: Tekton CI Pipeline Implementation

To get hands-on with Tekton, I designed and implemented a fully automated CI pipeline with no manual intervention.

Pipeline Workflow:

Developer commits code to GitHub.

A webhook (via ngrok) triggers the Tekton pipeline automatically.

Tekton clones the repository, builds the Docker image, and pushes it to Docker Hub.

The image is tagged using the Git commit ID for version tracking.

This setup completed the CI part — from code commit to image push — seamlessly and automatically.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔸 Task 4: CD Pipeline with FluxCD

Once the new image was pushed to the registry, FluxCD automatically detected the update and:

Pulled the latest image from Docker Hub.

Deployed the new version into the Kubernetes cluster.

Removed the old version after successful deployment.

This created a complete CI/CD pipeline — Tekton handling the build and Flux handling deployment.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔸 Task 5: Full Automation with Terraform

The next challenge was to automate the entire microservices deployment workflow using Terraform.

Here’s what I achieved:

Fetched microservice source code from Azure DevOps repositories.

Created individual GitHub repositories for each microservice automatically.

Added Tekton pipeline files and webhooks to each repo.

Automated the build, image push to OpenShift internal registry, and deployment to the OpenShift cluster — all through Terraform.

This resulted in end-to-end automation — from source code download to deployment — using Terraform as the orchestrator.

